# We cannot diectly access a container within a pod. We need to expose the pod for that. We use services in kubernetes to expose pods. Simply Service is a method for exposing pods in your cluster.

# 4 types of services. namely, ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer and ExternalName

ClusterIP: Available only within the cluster. Minly used for database pods, since we do not want to expose databases to have external access

# svc manifest file for clusterIP:

---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: svc-name
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  selector:
    app: swiggy
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 80

# Nodeport: this svc help-s to access the pods from the internet

# The ports that can be exposed via NodePort has a range. which is 30000 to 32767. if we don't mention the port, it will be assigned randomly. we can assign the nodeport as well. but it should be within the range.

---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: node-name
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: swiggy
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 80
      nodePort: 30002

# Here the drawback is all the requests will go toa single node. becoz the pod is accessed through the pod's public IP and via the mentioned port.

# This is solved by using Load Balancer

# Load Balancer distributes or routes the traffic(requests) among the servers

---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: lb-name
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: swiggy
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 80
      nodePort: 30003

| kubectl describe svc svc-name |

| kubectl delete svc svc-name |












